  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nAs an upper low slides over southern Alberta and northern Montana\nthis morning, look for the chance of precipitation to diminish\nuntil later today when the low slides far enough east to bring\nprecipitation back to the mountains. In the meantime, look for\nincreasing westerly synoptic winds across the area. Gusts in the\n20s and 40s mph will be common across southern Montana, with gusts\ninto the low 50s mph possible over the foothills west of Billings.\nTonight into Thursday morning, these windy conditions will persist\nthrough the morning hours, before shifting east and out of the\narea through the afternoon/evening timeframe.\n\nThe aforementioned increasing chance of precipitation will come in\nthe form of showers and weak thunderstorms, mainly along and south\nof Interstate 90/94 with the greatest chance in the mountains.\nTonight, a shortwave drops through the region on the back side of\nthe low, bringing a better chance of precipitation across the \narea through Thursday morning. While the chance is low (5 to 15 \npercent), embedded thunder is possible.\n\nSnow levels will be as low as 7,000 feet this morning, allowing \nlight snow accumulations in the mountains into Thursday. The \nprimary accumulations will be at the higher elevations of the \nAbsaroka/Beartooth and Bighorn Mountains. These showers may \nimpact mountain recreation and travel at times, including the \nhighest elevations of the Beartooth Highway in the Beartooth \nMountains and Highway 14 through the Bighorn Mountains.\n\nWhile conditions trend drier and warmer Friday, ensemble \nforecasts suggest additional waves of energy will drop into the \narea from the northwest this weekend into early next week, \nbringing cooler temperatures and the chance of precipitation back \nto the area. LA/PM",
